< Under an assumed posting delay $d\geq1$ hours after the end of a clock hour, the most recent usable completed hourly price at the start of hour $t$ is
< \begin{equation}\label{eq:cutoff}
<   \tau(t;d)=t-(d+1)\ \text{hours}.
< \end{equation}
< For the primary $d=1$, the most recent allowed hourly maximum is $y_{t-2}$: hour $t-1$ finishes only when the forecast for hour $t$ is issued and is not presumed published at that instant. A value indexed by an earlier observation hour is still treated as conditional on the assumed posting schedule and the final archived vintage.
< 
< \section{Models and Experimental Protocol}\label{sec:methods}
< \subsection{Two matched supervised forecasters}
< Let $x_t\in\mathbb R^p$ collect the legally gated price-history, realized-load, day-ahead, storage, and calendar features in \cref{tab:availability}. The baseline predicts $y_t$ from $x_t$ alone. The augmented model additionally receives six fixed \Chronos\ summaries $c_t\in\mathbb R^6$:
< \begin{align}
<  \hat y^{(0)}_{t,s}&=\sinh\!\left(f^{(0)}_s(x_t)\right),\\
<  \hat y^{(1)}_{t,s}&=\sinh\!\left(f^{(1)}_s(x_t,c_t)\right),\label{eq:models}
< \end{align}
< where $s\in\{0,1,2,3,42\}$ denotes the matched seed. The internal prediction is made on the $\operatorname{asinh}(y_t)$ scale and decoded into \$/\MWh. The training loss is absolute error on the transformed target. Both variants use \XGB\ histogram-based trees of maximum depth six, learning rate 0.05, the same split definitions, and 2023 early stopping. A final asinh-scale prediction bound is present in the implementation; its upper decoding level, $\sinh(10)\approx 11{,}013$, is not reached by the forecasts in the 2021 stress window. No model weights from \Chronos\ are fine-tuned.
< 
< \subsection{Frozen probabilistic features}\label{subsec:chronos}
< For each origin, the \Chronos--T5-small context consists of 512 hourly-max observations ending at $\tau(t;d)$. The corrected caches use a newly generated set of 20 sample trajectories. Because the intended target is hour $t$, the model samples $h=1+d$ future steps from that context, and the features summarize the step associated with $t$, not the first unobserved intermediate hour. Write these 20 forecast samples as $Z_{t}^{(1)},\dots,Z_{t}^{(20)}$. We construct
< \begin{equation}\label{eq:chronos_features}
<  c_t=\left(
<  \overline Z_t,\ q_{0.10,t},\ q_{0.50,t},\ q_{0.90,t},\
<  q_{0.90,t}-q_{0.10,t},\
<  \frac1{20}\sum_{m=1}^{20}\mathbf 1\{Z_t^{(m)}>200\}
<  \right),
< \end{equation}
< with empirical sample quantiles $q_{\alpha,t}$. The last component is a sample exceedance fraction relative to \$200/\MWh, not a calibrated event probability. It is included as an input feature and is not interpreted as a calibrated predictive distribution. Quantiles and means are forecast summaries, not observed prices. Crucially, they are recalculated for each delay scenario rather than shifting an earlier cache, and the prediction target hour does not move when a delay assumption changes.
< 
