< \subsection{Primary estimand and uncertainty}\label{subsec:inference}
< We average dollar-scale predictions across the five matched seeds before scoring, rather than average five separate MAEs:
< \begin{align}
<  \overline y_t^{(j)} &= \frac15\sum_{s\in\mathcal S}\hat y^{(j)}_{t,s}, \quad j\in\{0,1\},\\
<  \MAE_j &=\frac1N\sum_{t\in\mathcal T}\left|y_t-\overline y_t^{(j)}\right|,\\
<  \Delta_{\MAE} &= \MAE_1-\MAE_0. \label{eq:estimand}
< \end{align}
< The paired hourly loss differential is $\delta_t=|y_t-\overline y_t^{(1)}|-|y_t-\overline y_t^{(0)}|$. Negative $\Delta_{\MAE}$ favors augmentation. Our primary 95\% interval resamples the paired error series with 24-hour moving blocks and 2,000 bootstrap draws; the bootstrap random seed is fixed at zero. We additionally report a Diebold--Mariano comparison using autocorrelation-robust inference on the absolute-loss differential. Both inference methods retain the pairing of test hours and models. Block lengths of 12, 48, 72, and 168 hours are sensitivity checks, not alternative significance-search procedures.
< 
< Two distinct measures should not be conflated: $\MAE_j$ above is the error of the \emph{seed-averaged point forecast}, while $5^{-1}\sum_s\MAE(\hat y^{(j)}_{\cdot,s})$ is the \emph{mean individual-seed error}. Only the former defines the primary contrast. The \$200/\MWh\ spike threshold is a secondary, fixed descriptive diagnostic, not a threshold optimized on 2024.
< 
< \section{Results}\label{sec:results}
< \subsection{Primary matched ablation}\label{subsec:primary}
< The headline result is in \cref{tab:primary}. Adding frozen \Chronos\ summaries reduces ensemble MAE by \$0.15/\MWh\ (approximately 1.0\% of the \$15.07/\MWh\ baseline), while the two-sided 95\% interval includes zero. The bootstrap $p$-value is 0.131, and the Diebold--Mariano $p$-value is 0.124. These are consistent tests of the same substantive null: the observed point improvement does not provide sufficient evidence, at conventional thresholds, that the augmentation improves expected absolute-error performance in this retrospective setting. This is not evidence of exact equivalence or proof that \Chronos\ can never be useful. It is a failure to establish the incremental benefit sought in this experiment.
