< \subsection{Target completeness and alternative aggregation}
< A complete-target sensitivity excludes the final partially observed 2024 hour and refits both \XGB\ variants on the remaining 8,766 evaluation origins under identical model settings. The refitted predictions agree with the corresponding saved predictions, and the primary qualitative inference is unchanged. The one-hour edge observation is nevertheless retained in the previously fixed 8,767-hour headline, so the published analysis does not retroactively redefine its index.
< 
< Two secondary choices examine how strongly the result depends on the target and location. First, replacing the hourly maximum by the hourly \emph{mean} yields a \Chronos\ gap of \$-0.18/\MWh\ with nominal 95\% interval $[-0.28,-0.07]$. The \Chronos\ features still summarize predicted hourly maxima; this is a sensitivity to the supervised response, not a new primary experiment. Second, an HB\_NORTH analysis, using that hub's own delayed prices and independently generated \Chronos\ cache, yields a seed-averaged gap of \$-0.15/\MWh\ with nominal interval $[-0.29,-0.02]$. HB\_NORTH belongs to ERCOT and is not evidence of transfer across independent markets. Neither nominal secondary interval should supersede the primary result: these comparisons were not the designated primary hypothesis, and multiple examinations raise selection concerns.
< 
< \subsection{February 2021: ordinary accuracy does not imply tail robustness}\label{subsec:crisis}
< We perform a separate chronological stress evaluation of February 10--20, 2021, the period surrounding the Texas winter storm and widespread generation failures documented by the U.S. Federal Energy Regulatory Commission and the North American Electric Reliability Corporation \citep{ferc2021}. For this diagnostic, the models train only on pre-2021 history, reserve the last 90 days of 2020 for early stopping, and issue predictions on the 2021 series. This protocol is deliberately different from the principal 2019--2022 / 2023 / 2024 split and its errors must not be combined with the 2024 headline. The stress-window definition predates scoring the corrected forecasts, but it remains a single historically salient episode.
< 
< The crisis window has 264 scored hours. Actual hourly maximum prices reach \$9,051.55/\MWh. An exogenous-only \XGB\ model reaches at most \$697.21/\MWh\ in its saved crisis forecasts and incurs \$3,836.73/\MWh\ MAE. The \Chronos-augmented model reaches at most \$2,366.94/\MWh\ and incurs \$3,604.11/\MWh\ MAE. Both severely underestimate the realized scarcity prices. In contrast, the legal-lag persistence predictor $\hat y_t=y_{t-2}$ has crisis MAE \$540.13/\MWh\ (\cref{tab:crisis}). It uses the same target hours and the same assumed one-hour price-availability delay. It is not a contemporaneous or otherwise privileged baseline.
< 
