< This reversal was checked for common implementation explanations. The target hours align across models, the persistence lag is $t-2$, and none of the tree predictions reaches the implementation's $\sinh(10)$ upper decode bound. Replacing the transformed target with a raw-price tree did not remove the failure: its crisis MAE is approximately \$3,824/\MWh. Nor is the failure explained simply by an absence of very large prices in training: the pre-2021 training history includes an hourly maximum of \$9,005.29/\MWh\ on August 13, 2019 and ten hours above \$5,000/\MWh. These checks constrain explanations without proving a unique causal mechanism. A plausible interpretation is that the models' learned conditional predictions remain tied to ordinary covariate combinations, whereas a persistence rule mechanically transmits an extreme price immediately after its observation becomes admissible.
< 
< The reversal is specific. Outside the 264 crisis hours, exogenous \XGB\ achieves \$16.93/\MWh\ MAE and augmented \XGB\ \$17.20/\MWh, both lower than persistence at \$25.83/\MWh. Consequently, ``the best model'' depends both on the regime and on the intended loss. The strong crisis performance of persistence should not be generalized to all hours, and the crisis results should not be treated as evidence that the foundation-model features systematically cause or cure extreme-event failure.
< 
< \section{Discussion and Limitations}\label{sec:discussion}
< \paragraph{Incremental signal versus architectural novelty.} The principal estimand isolates the marginal contribution of six frozen probabilistic forecast summaries conditional on a reasonably rich tabular information set. The observed change is economically small in average per-MWh error and statistically unresolved. It would be inaccurate to interpret the non-rejection as evidence of formal equivalence; establishing equivalence would require a justified tolerance and an appropriately powered equivalence test. It would be equally inaccurate to claim that the foundation model is superior based on a negative point estimate alone. The target and feature choices may help explain the modest measured increment: autoregressive trees already use the same historical price series from which \Chronos\ forms its predictions, so its forecast summaries need not be independent sources of information. This is an interpretation, not an experimentally identified mechanism.
< 
< \paragraph{The source-vintage boundary.} We enforce explicit delays and source gates, but historical \emph{observation} time, first \emph{publication} time, and final \emph{archive} value are different objects. Real-time prices originate from a weekly settlement archive, not a captured first-publication feed. Their timestamps do not prove when those values became public, and a later archive revision can preserve the same observation timestamp while changing the number. The realized-load source is a daily archive with similarly unverified within-day availability. A one-hour delay therefore defines a controlled information-availability \emph{scenario}, not a verified 2019--2024 deployment record. Longer delays and removal of load probe the dependence on that scenario, but do not answer the archive-vintage question. Establishing fully operational validity would require historical publication timestamps and as-published data versions.
